@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  OREXIS SENSING, AGENT 0.2.0.
#  The sensing layer of Agent 0.2.0 speaks SOSA and SSN, and declares only what they lack. A
#  sensor sosa:observes a property and sosa:isHostedBy what it is mounted in — the feature of
#  interest, or a sosa:Sample that sosa:isSampleOf one — its board reaches it by ssn:hasSubSystem,
#  and how often it reports is its ssn-system:Frequency; a range is SSN-System's, stated by the
#  host, by what the host is a sample of, or by the sensor itself. Declared here is the graph an
#  observation is kept in, the silence of a sensor that has stopped reporting, the three sides
#  this layer's rules conclude, and the pipeline's own concepts, since neither standard says how
#  bytes become a number: a codec, the binding of a sensor to one, the member that ships, and the
#  pointer; and how a number becomes a quantity, which is a scaling and a calibration the world states,
#  and the rules. Sensing is the translation row of the agent stack and
#  nothing above it: a transport hands it bytes, it makes a number of them and writes one
#  sosa:Observation holding until the next is due, and on the container's tick it says which
#  readings are missing and which sensors have gone silent. It predicts nothing — that is the
#  prediction package's — and concludes nothing itself: the sides are revisions, concluded by the
#  three rules this layer ships (rules.ttl beside this file) and run by the deliberator over
#  the present. No word of any transport is spoken here.

:ObservationGraph a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:StateGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Observation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """One graph per feature and property, holding the whole sosa:Observation the
    sensor last made of it — feature, property, result, instant, sensor, procedure, and the
    instant the device says the result applies to where it says one — from its instant to the
    horizon its period states, replaced whole by the next. Named for what it holds. Beneath
    orexis:StateGraph so that the readers of the present find it by that kind today; that parent
    goes when what a plan may change is the actions' footprint rather than a graph kind.""" .

:ForecastGraph a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Forecast graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """One stretch of a forecast: the sosa:Observation a sensor reading a series made of
    one stretch ahead - of what, which property, the number, the instant it was issued, the sensor -
    holding during that stretch, and forgotten with the rest of that sensor's forecast when the
    next arrives. Named for what it holds. A belief and not a reading: not beneath
    orexis:StateGraph, so no plan forks it and no prediction takes it for the observation in hand.""" .

:Codec a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Codec" ;
    rdfs:comment """A wire format, both ways: how a sensor's bytes are a document and a document is
    bytes. The first stage of the pipeline, bytes → document → number, and a family
    in rule 2's sense: the members are interchangeable and a sensor is bound to one by
    :decodedBy. JSON ships with this layer; a member from a package declares its own term as an
    instance of this class and implements this layer's Codec contract, and genesis loads it by
    that term. Absent a binding, a sensor's bytes are JSON, which every board here speaks.""" .

:decodedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range :Codec ;
    rdfs:label "decoded by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The codec a sensor's bytes are read with — derived onto the sensor at genesis from what its device states." .

:Json a :Codec ;
    rdfs:label "JSON" ;
    rdfs:comment "The document is JSON, and a value in it is found by the pointer." .

:TwoPointScaling a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Two-point scaling" ;
    rdfs:comment """A RESCALE: the unit changes. How a sensor's number — a probe's ADC count — is a
    quantity in its property's unit — a soil-moisture fraction: the straight line through two points,
    each what the sensor reads there and what that stands for, named by its label (dry, wet).
    Unclamped, so a count past a point is a quantity past its own, which a side then says, rather
    than a saturated one hiding a flooded or frozen probe. The world states one per instrument it
    rescales (`scales`), beside the sensor; this layer's rule reads it at layer 0, and a sensor no
    scaling scales gives its number as its quantity — a thermometer already reports degrees.""" .

:TwoPointCalibration a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Two-point calibration" ;
    rdfs:comment """A CORRECTION: the unit stays. How a quantity an instrument reports with an error
    is the true one — a thermometer reading 0.4 in ice water and 99.1 in boiling — the straight line
    through two points, each what the sensor reads there in its own unit and what is true. Stated by
    the world beside the sensor it corrects, and read by this layer's rule at layer 1, after any
    scaling, over the scaled quantity; a sensor no calibration corrects keeps its scaled quantity as
    its reading.""" .

:scales a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :TwoPointScaling ;
    rdfs:range sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "scales" .

:corrects a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :TwoPointCalibration ;
    rdfs:range sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "corrects" .

:Point a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Point" ;
    rdfs:comment """One reference of a scaling or a calibration: what the sensor reads there (`reads`)
    and what that stands for (`standsFor`), named by its rdfs:label — dry, wet, ice — for whoever
    measures it again.""" .

:point a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range :Point ;
    rdfs:label "point" .

:reads a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Point ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "reads" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the sensor gives at a point: its raw number for a scaling, its scaled quantity for a calibration." .

:standsFor a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Point ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stands for" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a point's reading is: the quantity for a scaling, the true quantity for a calibration." .

:scaledResult a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "scaled result" ;
    rdfs:comment """The quantity in the property's unit, before any calibration corrects it: concluded
    at layer 0 from the number the sensor gave, through the sensor's scaling or as it is, and read at
    layer 1 to conclude the reading (sosa:hasSimpleResult). What a calibration's points read.""" .

:rawResult a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "raw result" ;
    rdfs:comment """The number a sensor gave, as the pointer found it in the bytes — all `received`
    writes of an observation beside who made it and when. What it is an observation OF, and the
    quantity (sosa:hasSimpleResult), this layer's rules conclude from the topology and the scaling
    and calibration the world states of the sensor.""" .

:readingPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reading pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment """Which value of a decoded document is this sensor's, as a JSON Pointer (RFC 6901).
    A fact about a document and not about a bus: two sensors on one board share a message and
    differ only here. The middle stage of the pipeline, document → raw value, and the one that
    is data rather than a member. Absent, the pointer is /value, which is what every
    single-property device here publishes.""" .

:startsPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "starts pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment """Where a decoded document keeps the instants a SERIES' values are for, as a JSON
    Pointer to an array of them, each the START of the stretch its value holds over, the next
    instant its end. A sensor stating it reads a series, its reading pointer finding the array of
    values, and writes a forecast graph per stretch rather than one observation.""" .

:endsPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "ends pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment """As startsPointer, the instants each the END of the stretch its value holds over,
    the one before its start: what a service summing the hour up to a time says of rain.""" .

:silentSince a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "silent since" ;
    rdfs:comment """The sensor has stopped reporting: its last reading fell due at this instant and
    nothing has arrived for a limit of its cadences since. A row present while the silence lasts,
    named for the state and not for the tick that noticed it, written by `missed` into a graph of
    the agent's own and dropped by the sensor's next reading. SOSA and SSN say how often a sensor
    reports and nothing about one that has stopped, which is why this is sensing's word.""" .

:below a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "below" ;
    rdfs:comment """The observation's result lies under the floor of this range — an
    ssn-system:OperatingRange or ssn-system:SurvivalRange stated for the observed property by what
    hosts the sensor, by what that is a sample of, or by the sensor itself. Concluded by this
    layer's rule as a revision, read by a desire's met-test, and written by an effect that predicts
    a reading. One triple per observation and range; nothing is minted per subject.""" .

:inside a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "inside" ;
    rdfs:comment "The observation's result lies within this range, its bounds included." .

:above a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "above" ;
    rdfs:comment "The observation's result lies over the ceiling of this range." .
